In article <237@scorn.sco.COM> davidbe@sco.COM (The Cat in the Hat) writes:
>rec.games.hack's own cs021104@cslab1c.UUCP (Curtis Yarvin) said:
>
>		    [ my whetsone ideas deleted ]
>
>-Vote against.  There are a number of criteria which, to me, are a litmus test
>-for any suggested Nethack addition. This one fails them all:
>-
>-1) Does it increase the tactical complexity of the game?  
>-The only logical response to finding a whetstone would be to
>-use it until it ran out; the only effect it would have is to help the player.
>
>So?  Maybe *you* can finish the game, but there are a number of people who 
>have a lot of trouble; even when the game isn't compiled with HARD.  Something
>to help the player would be nice.  And if you use the stone incorrectly, it's
>not going to help.
>
>-2) Does it redress a general tactical imbalance in Nethack?  Many previous
>-posters have suggested that one exists in 3.0.  Namely, it's too hard.
>-Whetstones might help this; but they wouldn't help much.  You'd need a +6
>-whetstone just to get a +3 weapon:
>

The point I was trying to make was that it's very easy to help the player;
therefore, it's best to do this (and I _do_ think it should be done) with
something that increases tactical complexity.  Whetstones don't.

>-The necessity of polymorphing _is_ a serious tactical imbalance, and 
>-hopefully will be eliminated in 3.1.  
>
>I don't object to the need for polymorphing *so* much.  I'd like to see it
>made impossible to get named weapons via polymorph, though (I've heard it's
>possible; I've never been lucky enough to do it).

Polymorphing is _boring_.  It takes a while to walk up & down through the
dungeon and find all the objects that could become something nice.  Save your
stronghold-wishing for the Stormbringer/Excalibur.

>In general, something that helps a beginning character survive is a good
>thing.  And those who don't think a +3 weapon is necessary haven't gotten
>to level 6 without finding either a longsword, a wand of polymorph, or an
>altar.

Didn't say it wasn't necessary; said it wasn't sufficient (to ensure an easy
victory.)  Not that it is necessary; good armor, for example, is even better.
But if it was, wouldn't it be better to give everybody one in the first
place?  Introducing yet another random-luck factor into Nethack doesn't seem
real desirable.

>One last question, regarding point 2:  Do wands have a MAX number of charges
>specific to type?  If so, the same kind of thing could be used with the
>whetstones.
>
>Just some thoughts...

Don't think so; charges are conserved through polymorph, the only exception
being wands of wishing, which cannot have more than 3 charges.  Without this,
it's very easy to get infinite wishes.

